# images that must never boot again, by authenticode sha-256.
#
# secure boot answers one question: was this signed by a key you hold. it has
# no notion of WHEN. every image vos has ever signed stays bootable forever,
# so an old release -- old kernel, old CVEs, old bugs -- can be dropped back
# onto the ESP and the firmware will run it with every signature checking out
# and every build gate green. this file is the only part of the chain that can
# say "not that one".
#
# add an entry with:  ./build.sh revoke path/to/old-signed.efi
# it is enrolled into dbx by ./build.sh dbx, which ./build.sh uki runs for you.
#
# these hashes are NOT sha256sum of the file -- see pehash.py. G15 proves the
# hash function agrees with the signatures it revokes, and A7 proves the
# firmware actually refuses a revoked image, because a dbx entry that matches
# nothing looks identical to one that works.
#
# entries are permanent. removing one un-revokes a known-bad image.
#
# format:  <64 hex chars>  <what it was, and why it is dead>
